  "id": "irish_showband",
  "name": "Showband (Irish ballroom dance bands)",
  "family": "pop",
  "lineage": "Seven- or eight-piece touring dance bands that filled Irish ballrooms through the 1960s, a style begun in the 1950s by the Clipper Carlton of Strabane, who stood and put on a show instead of sitting behind music stands. A front line of trumpet, trombone and saxophone with electric guitar, bass, drums and keyboards backed a lead singer through covers of current pop, rock and roll and country hits; the Royal Showband's 'The Hucklebuck' (1965) and Dickie Rock and the Miami Showband's singles reached number one in Ireland.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1960s showband, functional Song, pop rock branch. thick, solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. schilke chicago lead bright trumpet, trombone, saxophone, clean strummed single coil electric guitar, electric bass, machine hammered symmetric pattern organized rows full lathed both sides lathed drum kit. live tracking. Telefunken ELA M 251, tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips plate reverb.",
